year><volume>37</volume><fpage>58</fpage><lpage>66</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19106183</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="CIT59"><label>59</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ibrahim</surname><given-names>MM</given-names></name><name><surname>Razmara</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Nguyen</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Donahue</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Wubah</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Knudsen</surname><given-names>TB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Altered expression of mitochondrial 16S ribosomal RNA in p53-deficient mouse embryos revealed by differential display</article-title><source>Biochim Biophys Acta</source><year>1998</year><volume>1403</volume><fpage>254</fpage><lpage>64</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9685670</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="CIT60"><label>60</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bakhanashvili</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Grinberg</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Bonda</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Simon</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Moshitch-Moshkovitz</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Rahav</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>p53 in mitochondria enhances the accuracy of DNA synthesis</article-title><source>Cell Death Diff</source><year>2008</year><volume>15</volume><fpage>1865</fpage><lpage>74</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="CIT61"><label>61</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kuroiwa</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mitochondrial nuclei</article-title><source>Int Rev Cytol</source><year>1982</year><volume>75</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>59</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6179895</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list><sec id="sec1-6"><title>AUTHOR'S PROFILE</title><graphic xlink:href="JC-08-50893-g007.jpg" position="float"/><p><bold>Dr. Singh</bold> is one of the leaders in the field of mitochondria research and medicine. He is Professor of Oncology at Roswell Park Cancer Institute, the first cancer research, treatment and education center established in the United States. Prior to joining Roswell Park Cancer Institute, he was an Assistant Professor of Oncology and Environmental Health at Johns Hopkins School of Medicine. He is an expert in mitochondrial genetics and has more than 20 years of research experience. Dr. Singh is the author of over 65 research publications, including three books entitled Mitochondrial DNA Mutations in Aging, Disease and Cancer, Oxidative Stress, Disease and Cancer and Mitochondria and Cancer.</p><p>Dr Singh founded the Mitochondria Research Society, is the founding editor-in-chief of the journal Mitochondrion and is instrumental in promoting Mitochondrial Research and Medicine around the world. Dr. Singh has served or serves on the editorial boards of Cancer Biology &#x00026; Therapy, Cancer Genomics and Proteomics, Technology in Cancer Research and Treatment, Nanomedicine, FEMS Yeast Research and Molecular Cancer and Journal of Oncology. He also serves as an expert peer reviewer for several other journals. He has served or serves as an expert panel grant of reviewers for U.S. Department of State, United Mitochondrial Disease Foundation, Komen Breast Cancer Foundation, and National Institutes of Health and other funding agencies such as National Research Council of Canada.</p></sec></back></article>